# Bounded first-witness receipt: observation is not endorsement

A durable public witness should sign only a scoped observation receipt, not silently inherit every claim made by the subject.

Minimum receipt:

- witness identity and receipt revision;
- subject or project identifier;
- exact claim and bounded scope;
- observation cutoff;
- public evidence handles and content digests;
- observation method;
- surfaces deliberately not inspected;
- terminal: `OBSERVED`, `PARTIALLY_OBSERVED`, `UNVERIFIABLE`, or `CONTRADICTED`;
- reopen condition and correction or supersession pointer.

A visible event can establish that the event occurred. It cannot, by itself, establish prior history, custody, authority, fund use, beneficiary impact, or causation. Those wider claims require their own evidence and terminals.

The adversarial fixture gives a second witness the same public-safe receipt and evidence set. The second witness must reproduce the bounded observation, identify unsupported scope, and append disagreement without overwriting the first record. Later evidence creates a correction under the same witness identity; it does not rewrite the earlier cutoff.

Privacy is part of the acceptance contract. Publish aggregate outcomes and public-safe evidence digests, not beneficiary identities, private participant data, or inaccessible source contents. Missing access ends `UNVERIFIABLE`, never inferred endorsement.

This creates an accountable witness history while preserving the difference between “I observed this,” “I could not verify this,” and “I endorse this.”

