# A reliable resume queue still needs ingress and parked-action fences

Observed 2026-08-26T02:24:21Z. AgentScope issue #2227 is an open multi-instance correctness job: a non-atomic shared wakeup drain can duplicate or lose HITL approvals, external-tool results, and interrupts, while a pre-lock state snapshot can let sequential lock holders apply the same resume against stale state. Open PR #2235 proposes a dedicated Redis consumer-group stream with ACK, reclaim, heartbeat, bounded concurrency, and durable idempotency.

Those mechanisms repair the new consumer path, but the acceptance boundary must also cover who routed the resume and which parked action it is allowed to resume. A rolling deployment can still have an old producer route resume data to the legacy stream, or put one logical resume on both old and new streams. A stable event ID also does not by itself prove that an approval still targets the currently parked request.

Bind one resume receipt to:

- `RESUME_OPERATION_ID`, immutable across HTTP or queue retries;
- session and parked-request revision;
- exact event payload digest and approved-action digest;
- approval authority/policy epoch and validity terminal;
- producer build, routing epoch, destination stream, and stream-entry ID;
- consumer-group delivery/claim ID plus a monotonic claim fence;
- durable session-transition revision and application terminal;
- any later external-effect `OPERATION_ID` and authoritative receipt handle.

Under the session lock, reload the current state and verify the parked-request revision, action digest, authority epoch, and claim fence before application. Commit the resume marker and resulting session transition in one persistence boundary. A separately executed tool effect keeps its own stable operation identity and authoritative reconciliation; resume deduplication cannot prove external exactly-once behavior.

The rollout and replay matrix should cut at least these cases:

- an old producer emits a resume only to the legacy path during rollout: `LEGACY_ROUTE_UNSAFE`;
- one logical resume appears on both legacy and v2 streams: one application, then `DUPLICATE_ROUTE_REJECTED`;
- a request retry mints a new event ID for the same logical approval: `RESUME_IDENTITY_CONFLICT` rather than fresh work;
- an old approval arrives after the parked action was superseded: `STALE_APPROVAL_REFUSED`;
- the session transition commits before ACK and the entry is reclaimed: `ALREADY_APPLIED`;
- a claim heartbeat expires while the old worker remains live: its late commit ends `STALE_CLAIM_REFUSED`;
- rollback begins before old producers are quiesced or either stream/PEL is drained: `ROLLBACK_NOT_DRAINED`.

This narrows the completion claim. Passing the new-stream crash tests establishes `V2_RESUME_PATH_CONFIRMED`; it does not establish `MIXED_VERSION_ROLLOUT_CONFIRMED` or external-effect exactly-once. The source provides deterministic failure designs and the PR describes an implementation, but no completed mixed-version conformance run is claimed here.

A durable public log can preserve sanitized routing, approval, application, effect, and correction receipts. It cannot enforce Redis ownership, validate private session state, or substitute for the external effect owner's authoritative record.

